* Introduction

	This guide describes features that are specific to ABCL/R2;
i.e., new/modified features compared to ABCL/1 [2]. The reader is
assumed to have read ``ABCL/1 User's Guide,'' which appears as an
Appendix of [2].

	ABCL/R2 is an object-oriented concurrent language with
reflective facilities.	As an object-oriented concurrent language, it
has most of features of ABCL/1 (the non-reflective predecessor of
ABCL/R2).  Furthermore, it supports reflective computations based on
Hybrid Group Architecture proposed in [1]. The semantic model of
ABCL/R2 is described in [1].

* Differences between ABCL/R2 and ABCL/1

	The ABCL/R2 system is not completely compatible with the
ABCL/1 system.	Some features of ABCL/1 are modified in ABCL/R2, and
some are restricted or not available.  Below, such features are
discussed.

** Unsupported features

	In this version of ABCL/R2, following features are not
available.  (Some of them may become available in the future version,
but some are not.)

*** Wait-For, Wait-For-Loop forms

	Wait-For and Wait-For-Loop are the forms to wait for specified
messages, but they are not supported.

*** Match-Loop form
	Match-Loop is a form that repeats the Match form, but not
supported.

*** Synchronous Message Transmission form

	Synchronous Message Transmission is a form to wait for replies
for multiple messages.  For example, following expression sends the
messages to motorX and motorY, then waits replies from motorX and
motorY.

	{[motorX <== [:step 30]] [motorY <== [:step 20]]}

	Current version of ABCL/R2 does not support this form.

*** Routine Call

	Routine Call is a way to recursion or code sharing within an
object.  Current version of ABCL/R2 system does not support this.

*** Some Common Lisp special forms

	In current version of ABCL/R2, following Common Lisp special
forms are SUPPORTED.

	DECLARE		FUNCTION	IF
	LET		LET*		PROGN
	QUOTE		SETQ

Some special forms, which are supported in ABCL/1, do not appear in
above list (e.g., TAGBODY/GO, BLOCK/RETURN, and multiple-value).  They
are not available.  However, the DO form, which is a macro in Common
Lisp, is supported as a special form in the ABCL/R2 system.

	Current version of ABCL/R2 ignores the contents of the declare
form.

*** Suicide form

	The suicide form, which terminates the execution of an object,
is not supported.

*** Constraints in Match form

	In ABCL/R2, the match form cannot handle the constraints.  For
example:
	Good:
	     (match x
	       (is 0 !t)
	       (is n !(1- n)))
	NG:
	     (match x
	       (is n where (zerop n)
		   !t)
	       (is n
		   !(1- n)))

** Features with Restricted Functionality

*** Express Mode Messages and Atomic form

	To send messages in the `express mode,' the customized
evaluator and metaobject generator should be used.  The library file
`express.abclr2' provides the definitions for them.

	Moreover, there are two restrictions. (1) An object that will
send an express message (i.e., the sender) must use an evaluator
`eval/express,' which can be created by an object `eval/express-gen.'
(2) The metaobject of the receiver must be created by a metaobject
generator `meta/express-gen.'

	An express message is launched by one of the following
expressions:
	[<receiver> <= <message> :express]
	[<receiver> <= <message> @ <reply-dest.> :express]
	[<receiver> <== <message> :express]

	An express message causes preemption of a script execution
that is invoked by a normal message.  The ATOMIC form prevents the
preemption during the execution of its subexpressions.  In these
sense, this express facility is compatible to ABCL/1's except its
syntax.

	Examples of express messages can be found in file
`examples/misc/express-ex.abclr2'.

*** Lambda Form

	Inside a lambda form embedded in a script, access to lexical
variables is not allowed.  (So it creates an anonymous function rather
than a lambda-closure...)  Also, a lambda form cannot have
ABCL-specific forms such as the message transmission and object
creation.

NG:
	[object foo
	 (state [x := 0])
	 (script
	  (=> [:test l]
	      !(member l :test #'(lambda (y) (= x y)))))]
	;; The variable `x' cannot be accessed in the lambda form.

Good:
	[object foo
	 (state [x := 0])
	 (script
	  (=> [:test l]
	      !(member l :test
		       (funcall #'(lambda (w)
				    #'(lambda (y) (= w y))
				    x)))))]
	;; Nevertheless, the lexical variable `w' can be accessed
	;; because it is bound in the lambda form.

*** Where Clause

	To define an object that has a `where clause' in its
script-pattern, the user should load the library `heavy-meta' before
the evaluation.  When a `where clause' is used in an object
definition, the compiler sets the metaobject generator of the object
to `heavy-meta'.

	The pattern-match algorithm is like the following pseudo-code.
Note that a message having no matching script is discarded, while it
is kept in ABCL/1.

    (let ((MRS <a triplet <Message, Reply, Sender> dequeued from the queue>)
	  (matched? nil))
      (dolist (script <a list of scripts>)
	(if (and <Does the pattern of script match with MRS?>
		 <Is the constraint satisfied?>)
	  (progn <execute the body of script>
		 (setq matched? t)
		 <return-from dolist-loop>)))
      (if (not matched?)
	  <discard MRS>))

** Features Given by the Library

*** Multicast

	To send multicast messages, an evaluator should be created by
an object `multicast-eval-gen,' which is defined in the library
`multicast.'

	Tips: The following expression enables multicast message
sending from the top-level.  This is because the top-level expression
is evaluated by an object bound to the variable `*eval*.'
	[*eval* := [multicast-eval-gen <== [:new *eval*]]]

*** Future Type Message

	The user should load the `future' library before the use of a
future type message.

** New Features and Modified Features

*** Loading and Compiling Programs

 (INC pathname &key (use-source nil) (verbose t) (compile nil))
							   [special form]

	A ABCL/R2 program specified by the argument `pathname' is
translated into Lisp program, then loaded.  When the program is
already translated into Lisp, and the value of keyword `use-source' is
nil, translation is skipped.  When the value of the keyword `compile'
is non-nil, the program is loaded after the translated Lisp program is
compiled.

	The environment and group where the program is evaluated are
the same ones where the INC form is evaluated.

	The argument `pathname' consists of the directory and the
(file) name.  The type should not be included.  For example, a file
"/foo/bar/baz.abclr2" is loaded by an expression:
	(inc "/foo/bar/baz")

 (COMP pathname &key (use-source nil) (verbose t))	       [macro]

	A ABCL/R2 program is translated into a Lisp program, then
compiled into a fasl file.  When the program is already translated or
compiled, these phases are skipped.  When the value of the keyword
use-source is non-nil, existence of translated program is ignored.

	File types are given in following manner:
	ABCL/R2 file:	"abclr2"
	Lisp file:	"lsp"
	Fasl file:	(depends on underlying Lisp system)

*** Loading Library

 (INC-LIB pathname &key (use-source nil) (verbose t) (compile nil))
								[special form]

	An ABCL/R2 program is loaded.  The environment and the group,
where the program is evaluated, are the top-level ones.

 (REQUIRE module-name &key (use-source nil) (verbose t) (compile nil))
								[macro]

	An ABCL/R2 program specified by the argument module-name is
loaded by INC-LIB when the value of module-name is not found in the
variable *modules*.  The program is searched in directories given by
the variable *library-directories*.
	When the module-name appears in *modules*, it loads nothing.

 (PROVIDE module-name)						[macro]

	The module-name is added into the variable *modules*.

Note: the value of the argument module-name can be either symbol or
string.

 *MODULES*							[variable]

	List of module names loaded.

 *LIBRARY-DIRECTORIES*						[variable]

	List of directory names.  Macro REQUIRE uses this variable to
search a library program given by name.

*** Current Directory

 (CD &optional pathname)					[function]

	The current directory is moved to a directory specified by the
argument `pathname.'  If the argument is not specified, the home
directory is selected.

 (PWD)								[function]

	The current directory is returned.

*** Continuation Object

	The ABCL/R2 system provides a special form to create an object
used as a continuation.	 We call such an object a continuation object.
A continuation object is created by the following form:

	[cont <pattern> <body>]

	The evaluation of this form creates a continuation object that
has same behavior to the one created by the form
	[object (script (=> <pattern> <body>))].
The only difference is that the former is much more efficiently
executed than the latter.

Note: The <body> part of a continuation object should not have
expressions whose execution time can not be predicted (e.g., loops or
now-type messages).  If it has, there may be a dead-lock.  The <body>
part can not be the subject of reflective computation, since it is
compiled as a script of a non-reifying object.

*** Toplevel

Most of toplevel commands of ABCL/1 (e.g., inspect) are not supported
in ABCL/R2 for some reasons.  Some commands (e.g., pwd, cd, inc, and
comp) are provided as functions/macros; some (e.g., trace-objects and
history) can be substituted by the reflective libraries; and some
(e.g., inspect) are not impremented yet.

Following two commands are the only ones provided as a toplevel
command in ABCL/R2:
	:q	quits the system.
	:reset	restarts the system.

The error handler of ABCL/R2 has many corner cuttings.  In the current
version, when an error is occured, the error message at Lisp-level is
displayed(*).  Thereafter, the system continues its execution as long
as possible.  In most cases, the system may complain because there is
no reply message for a present-type message.  Some system may invoke
the debugger at Lisp-level at the point of (*).  In this cane, the
exit from the debugger continues the execution of the system.

*** Lexical Variables

	Lexical variables for an object are the variables that are
defined at an outer environment of the object.  In ABCL/1, the values
of lexical variables are COPIED at the object-creation time.  After an
object is created, in the other words, any modification on a lexical
variable by the created object does not propagate to the original
variable, and vice versa.  In ABCL/R2, on the other hand, lexical
variables are SHARED among objects.  

	To contrast this difference, let us look the following
program.  In ABCL/1, the modification of n by the execution of "[n :=
(+ n 1)]" is NOT propagated to objects foo and baz.  In ABCL/R2, since
the variable n is SHARED, the result depends on the order of
execution.

	[object foo
	 (script
	  (=> [:test n]
	    (let ((bar [object (script (=> [:inc] [n := (+ n 1)]))])
		  (baz [object (script (=> [:get] !n))]))
	      [bar <= [:inc]]
	      !(list n [baz <== [:get]]))))]
	[foo <== [:test 10]]

	==> (10 10) ; in ABCL/1
	==> Either (10 10), (10 11), (11 10) or (11 11) ; in ABCL/R2

Authors are planning to change this feature to be COPY semantics in
future.

*** Event Handling

	Asynchronous events (SIGNALs in the Unix's terminology), such
as timer or I/O request, can be received by an object in ABCL/R2.
Here, we describe the interface for the event handling mechanism,
which consists of two layers: (1) event generation at Lisp level, and
(2) event receiving at ABCL level.

(1) Lisp Level:

When an event is occurred, the event handler at the Lisp level calls a
LISP function R2SYS:SEND-SIGNAL with some arguments.  Eventually, the
event is delivered to an ABCL object as a message.  (Alternatively, the
variable R2SYS:*SIGNAL-REQUEST* is changed to be a non-nil value, a
message is sent to an ABCL object.  This interface is for systems that
cannot call LISP functions at the event handlers.)

(2) ABCL Level:

Once an event is notified to the ABCL system at the Lisp level, it is
notified to an ABCL object by a message.  An event handler object,
which is the one bound to the global variable *SIGNAL-HANDLER*, is the
receiver.  Received messages have a form like this: [:signal
<arguments> ...], whose arguments are the ones for function
R2SYS:SEND-SIGNAL.  Note that kind of events is only identified by the
arguments of the message.

By default, a signal handler, which ignores  any kinds of events are
defined.  When the value of variable *SIGNAL-HANDLER* is NIL, no
messages are sent.

* Reflective Computation in ABCL/R2

Though reflective computations in ABCL/R2 are based on the Hybrid
Group Architecture proposed in [1], some features are
extended/modified.  These features are described below.

** Compiler

	In current version of ABCL/R2, the script of the object is
partially compiled as is shown in [3].  As a result, the definition of
the default evaluator differs from the one presented in [1].  The user
should note following points:

  (a) The evaluator can receive a message like [:do Exp Env Id Gid
	Eval].  In such a message, Exp is a partially compiled
	expression and its form is [<tag> <body>].
  (b) The body part of an expression [:compiled <body>] is a Lisp
	lambda-closure whose arguments are continuation, environment,
	object ID, and evaluator. 
  (c) Parameters in the evaluator expression are already evaluated.  An
	ABCL/R2 expression `[x <= y @ z]' is evaluated at the
	meta-level by the message 
	   [:do [:send-past Target Message Reply] Env Id Gid Eval]
	to the evaluator.  Here, parameters Target, Message, and Reply
	have the run-time values of x, y, and z.
 

** Built-in Objects

*** Kernel Objects

	Usually, the system automatically creates the group kernel
objects (the metaobject generator, the evaluator, and the group
manager).  In file `examples/misc/MCP.abclr2,' there are definitions
of `MC-meta-gen,' `MC-eval,' and `Meta-circular.'  They correspond to
the definitions for the automatically-created group kernel objects.

*** Script Object

	A compiled script can be accessed as an object from the user
program.  The messages that the script object can receive are
described in file `examples/misc/built-ins.abclr2.'

	Furthermore, the user can define the customized script
objects. Files `examples/misc/user/script.abclr2' and
`lib/heavy-meta.abclr2' are the examples.

*** Global Objects

	At the startup time, following objects are already defined as
global objects.	 Their behaviors are described in file
`examples/misc/built-ins.abclr2.'
	`env-gen'	generates environment objects.
	`queue-gen'	generates queue objects.
	`global-env'	is the global environment object.

** Additional Parameters

	As is mentioned before, the current version of ABCL/R2 system
partially compiles the scripts of the user-defined objects.  To
customize the behavior of expressions, the compiler accepts additional
parameters for some forms.

	For example, the evaluation of an expression `[x <= y @ z]'
--- past-type message --- yields a message
	[:send-past Target Message Reply] 
to the evaluator at the meta-level where the values of Target,
Message, and Reply are the run-time values of x, y, and z.  Here, the
evaluation of an expression `[x <= y @ z :RVT s]' which has additional
parameters (:RVT and s) yields a message
	[:send-past Target Message Reply :RVT s*]
to the evaluator. (The value of s* is the run-time value of s.)  Even
in some library modules, these additional parameters are used (e.g.,
express message).

	Current version of ABCL/R2 system accepts such additional
parameters only in (1) the message transmission, (2) the object
definition, and (3) the assignment.

*** Message Transmission

Following expressions:

	[E1 <= E2 @ E3 A1 ... An]
	[E1 <== E2 A1 ... An]

yield messages to the evaluator:

	[:send-past E1* E2* E3* A1* ... An*]
	[:send-now E1* E2* A1* ... An*]

where Ei*, Ai* are the run-time values of Ei, Ai.

*** Object Creation

Following object creation form:

	[object Name
	 (meta-gen MGen)
	 (group GMgr)
	 (Tag1 Exp1)
	 (Tag2 Exp2)
	      :
	 (TagN ExpN)
	 (state [var := initValue])
	 (script ...)]

yields a message to the evaluator:

	[:object-def 'Name MGen* GMgr* StateSpec* ScriptSet* 
			:Tag1 Arg1 :Tag2 Arg2 ... TagN ArgN]


where MGen*, GMgr*, and Argi are the run-time values of MGen, GMgr,
and Expi; and StateSpec* and ScriptSet* are the compiled values of the
state and script parts of the expression.

Example:

An expression:

	[object foo
	  (priority N)
	  (script ...)]

yields a message to the evaluator

	[:object-def 'foo nil nil nil <list of Scripts> :priority 10]

where the run-time value of N is 10.

*** Assignment

Following assignment form:

	[Var := Exp A1 ... An]

yields a message to the environment object:

	[:set 'Var Exp1* A1* ... An*]

where Exp1* and Ai* are the run-time values of Exp and Ai.

** Special form REIFY-ARGS

In some reflective languages, such as 3-Lisp and ABCL/R, so called
reifying procedure is available.  In ABCL/R2, however, such a
procedure cannot be realized. This is because that the semantics of
the reifying procedures requires the user program to be interpreted,
and this requirement conflicts with our partial compilation scheme.

Though the all functionality of the reifying procedure cannot be
supplied, but special form REIFY-ARGS supplies some of them.

 (reify-args         KEYWORD EXP1 ... EXPN)
 (reify-args-to-meta KEYWORD EXP1 ... EXPN)		[Special Form]

When this special form is executed, KEYWORD is evaluated at first.
Hereafter, a message:

	[KEYWORD* EXPS ENV ID GID EVAL] @ C

is sent to an evaluator (in case of reify-args) or the metaobject of an
object whose script is being executed (in case of reify-args-to-meta).
Where KEYWORD* is the evaluated value of KEYWORD, EXPS is a list of
compiled objects of EXP1 to EXPN, and other parameters are context for
the evaluation of this form.

When this special form is appeared in the script of an non-reifying
object, it will cause a compile-time error.

The reader can see how to use this special form in file
reify-args.abclr2 at directory examples/misc.

